This Cisco packet tracer is supposed to simulate a small office set up.

Scenario: A small business with three departments (Admin, Sales, Guest) sharing one internet connection.

Topology:
- 1 router (e.g., ISR 4331) connected to a simulated ISP
- 2 Layer 2 switch (2960)
- 2–3 PCs per VLAN, plus a server (DHCP or web)

Client request: 

From: Dana Reyes, Owner, Reyes Supply Co.
To: Ethan Trombley, IT Contractor
Subject: Network setup for the new office

Hi Ethan,

The cabling’s done and the equipment is in place, so here’s what we need from the network.

Departments

- Sales (3 computers) and Admin (2 computers) each need their own separate network. Admin handles payroll and customer financials, so I want it kept apart from everyone else.
- The two departments still need to be able to reach each other when necessary.
- Guests in the lobby need Wi-Fi. It has to be password-protected with modern encryption. Guests must not be able to reach any of our department computers or equipment, under any circumstances.

Addresses

- Nobody here is going to type in IP settings by hand. Every computer and guest device should get its address automatically.
- Please give me a simple addressing plan I can keep on file.

Security

- Our last IT person left the equipment with no passwords. I want every device password-protected, and any stored passwords shouldn’t be readable in plain text.
- Remote management of the equipment must be encrypted. Only Admin should be able to log in remotely. Sales and guests shouldn’t even be able to try.
- Anyone logging in should see a warning that access is restricted.
- If someone unplugs one of our office computers and plugs in their own laptop, that port should shut itself off.
- Any network ports we’re not using should be disabled, so nobody can just plug in.

Reliability: 

- I paid for two cables between the switches. I’d like both of them doing work, not one sitting idle.

Deliverables
When you’re done, please send me:

1. The addressing plan
2. Proof that guests can’t reach our internal computers
3. Proof that remote management works from Admin and is blocked from everywhere else

Thanks,
Dana